//! member: true
//!
//! std.fmt — the text side of every value: padding, alignment, integers
//! in every base, and the text→number boundary. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **Why this module exists at all, given `{x}`.** Every string literal
//! in wolf is an f-string (X9/D26) and `"{n}"` already renders an `int`,
//! so a formatting module has to earn its place. It earns it twice. The
//! first reason is that the interpolation grammar reserves a FORMAT SPEC
//! — `INTERP ::= '{' expr FORMAT_SPEC? '}'` (spec/01 §1.5) — whose
//! semantics spec §7.4 was supposed to define and does not (F-0033) —
//! though the IMPLEMENTATIONS have converged: re-measured at the sc08 pin,
//! `{x:.2}`, `{s:.4}` and `{n:08}` render identically on all three lanes
//! (`3.14`, `wolf`, `00000042`), including precision on a `str`, which
//! F-0031 recorded as the one clause nothing implemented. F-0031's
//! divergence — lupin padding while wolfc ignored the spec — is retired.
//! The second is that a spec is a spelling, not a capability: a
//! program that computes a column width, or pads a value it received as
//! data, needs the operation as a FUNCTION. This module is that
//! function set, and it is also the running reference implementation the
//! §7.4 amendment cites (F-0033) — the spec text and these bodies are
//! meant to agree clause for clause.
//!
//! **Width is measured in BYTES** (§9's one currency, applied). `len` is
//! bytes everywhere in std, so `pad_left("é", 3)` adds one byte of fill,
//! not two, and a column of CJK text will not line up in a terminal. This
//! is honest rather than convenient: display width needs East Asian Width
//! tables and grapheme segmentation (the F-0019 budget decision), and a
//! function that pretended to count columns would be wrong on exactly the
//! text that makes people care. When the tables land, the display-width
//! family joins them under its own name — `pad_left` will keep meaning
//! bytes.
//!
//! **What is the language's, the implementation's, and std's** (§8's
//! question again). The language owns interpolation and its (unspecified)
//! format spec; the implementation owns `repeat`, `starts_with`, `upper`
//! and the rendering of `{n}` for a builtin; std owns everything named
//! here. `to_str` delegates to interpolation and says so, because that
//! makes the interpolated rendering part of this function's contract.
//!
//! **The parse family is re-homed here** (the sprint contract's word).
//! sc03 shipped `parse_int`/`parse_float` in `std.str` because that is
//! where the boundary-safe probe was proven; the number boundary belongs
//! to the module that also owns printing, so that `to_str_base` and
//! `parse_int_base` can be read — and tested — as inverses. `std.str` no
//! longer declares them. The re-home refined two behaviors, both
//! deliberate:
//!
//! - a radix outside 2..36 now raises `base` rather than `parse`, because
//!   a bad radix is the CALLER's mistake and a bad digit run is the
//!   DATA's, and a caller that cannot tell them apart cannot report
//!   either one usefully (§2, §10's trap-versus-raise rule read one step
//!   further);
//! - the accumulator runs NEGATIVE, so `parse_int("-9223372036854775808")`
//!   is `int_min()` instead of an overflow trap. Positive `int_min()` has
//!   no value to be, and still traps.
//!
//! **This module's one blocked contract is code as of sc10, and nothing
//! here is a contract now.** `truncate_to` could not be written for the
//! reason fifteen `std.str` functions could not: cutting a string at a
//! byte offset requires knowing the offset is a code-point boundary, the
//! only way to ask was to slice there, and slicing at a non-boundary
//! traps uncatchably (D30, F-0018). s37's `str.get` asks instead of
//! faulting, `std.str` spent that primitive in sc09, and sc10 spends it
//! here: the function is a walk down from `width` until the range is
//! accepted — three iterations at worst, because no UTF-8 scalar is wider
//! than four bytes — and it is at the bottom of this file.
//!
//! Float printing and parsing live in `std.fmt.float`: wolf has no
//! overloading, so one module cannot spell both `to_str(n: int)` and
//! `to_str(x: f64)` — the same split, for the same reason, as
//! `std.math`/`std.math.float` (§10).

/// The digit character for `d` in the 36-digit lowercase alphabet.
/// Private. Slicing an ASCII literal this module owns is always a
/// code-point boundary — the safe direction of sc03's inversion.
fn digit_char(d: int) -> str {
    let alphabet = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"
    alphabet[d..d + 1]
}

/// The value of the digit `s` starts with in `radix`, or -1 when `s` does
/// not start with one. Case-insensitive above radix 10. Private; the
/// public entry points are the `parse_*` family.
///
/// **One byte, one classification** (s77, the sc12 pin). This ran as
/// sc03's inversion until now — `starts_with` against a literal this
/// module owns, never a slice of the caller's input — which is what made
/// `parse_int("é")` a miss rather than a `bounds` trap while nothing could
/// read a byte (F-0018). It cost up to `2 * radix` probes per character,
/// so a base-36 parse asked seventy-two questions to answer one. The byte
/// view answers it directly, out of `s`'s own storage, and the ASCII ranges
/// below are the same alphabet `digit_char` renders, read backwards.
///
/// The byte is taken by ITERATING and returning rather than by indexing:
/// `s.bytes()[0]` is `unsupported` on wolfc's checked tier (F-0071), and a
/// shape that costs a lane is not one std writes. An empty `s` runs the
/// loop zero times and falls through to the miss, exactly as the
/// `starts_with` probe did.
///
/// Totality survives the change and its argument gets shorter: a byte is a
/// byte, so a multi-byte character's lead byte (195 for `é`) is simply not
/// a digit.
fn digit_of(s: str, radix: int) -> int {
    for c in s.bytes() {
        var v: int = 0 - 1
        if c >= 48 && c <= 57 {
            v = c - 48
        } else if c >= 97 && c <= 122 {
            v = c - 87
        } else if c >= 65 && c <= 90 {
            v = c - 55
        }
        if v >= radix {
            return 0 - 1
        }
        return v
    }
    0 - 1
}

/// `n` written in `radix` with a leading `-` when negative, assuming a
/// radix in 2..36. Private: the public `to_str_base` validates the radix
/// first.
///
/// The accumulator is NEGATIVE on purpose. Negating `int_min()` traps
/// (X3), so a digit loop that works on `0 - n` cannot render the smallest
/// `int` at all; running the loop on the non-positive side renders every
/// `int` there is. Both `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so the
/// remainder of a negative value is non-positive and `0 - v % radix` is
/// the digit.
fn digits(n: int, radix: int) -> str {
    if n == 0 {
        return "0"
    }
    var v = n
    if v > 0 {
        v = 0 - v
    }
    var out = ""
    while v < 0 {
        let d = 0 - v % radix
        let c = digit_char(d)
        out = "{c}{out}"
        v = v / radix
    }
    if n < 0 {
        return "-{out}"
    }
    out
}

/// `s` padded on the LEFT with spaces to at least `width` bytes —
/// right-alignment, the `{x:>w}` spec's meaning. A string already at
/// least `width` bytes long comes back unchanged: this function pads and
/// never truncates, so no data is ever lost to a column. A negative or
/// zero `width` is not an error, it simply pads nothing. Total: no error
/// row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.pad_left("42", 5) == "   42"
/// fmt.pad_left("42", 2) == "42"
/// fmt.pad_left("42", 0) == "42"
/// fmt.pad_left("", 3) == "   "
/// ```
pub fn pad_left(s: str, width: int) -> str {
    pad_left_with(s, width, " ")
}

/// `pad_left` with an explicit one-byte `fill`. Traps `assert` when
/// `fill` is not exactly one byte: a multi-byte fill cannot reach an
/// arbitrary byte width, and silently overshooting the column would make
/// the width promise a lie — the caller could have checked, so it is a
/// trap and not a row (§2).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.pad_left_with("42", 5, "0") == "00042"
/// fmt.pad_left_with("42", 5, ".") == "...42"
/// fmt.pad_left_with("42", 1, "0") == "42"
/// ```
pub fn pad_left_with(s: str, width: int, fill: str) -> str {
    if fill.len != 1 {
        assert(false)
    }
    let missing = width - s.len
    if missing <= 0 {
        return s
    }
    let pad = fill.repeat(missing)
    "{pad}{s}"
}

/// `s` padded on the RIGHT with spaces to at least `width` bytes —
/// left-alignment, the `{x:<w}` spec's meaning. Pads, never truncates.
/// Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.pad_right("42", 5) == "42   "
/// fmt.pad_right("42", 2) == "42"
/// fmt.pad_right("", 3) == "   "
/// ```
pub fn pad_right(s: str, width: int) -> str {
    pad_right_with(s, width, " ")
}

/// `pad_right` with an explicit one-byte `fill`; the `assert` trap on a
/// multi-byte fill is `pad_left_with`'s, for the same reason.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.pad_right_with("42", 5, "0") == "42000"
/// fmt.pad_right_with("ab", 4, "-") == "ab--"
/// ```
pub fn pad_right_with(s: str, width: int, fill: str) -> str {
    if fill.len != 1 {
        assert(false)
    }
    let missing = width - s.len
    if missing <= 0 {
        return s
    }
    let pad = fill.repeat(missing)
    "{s}{pad}"
}

/// `s` centered in `width` bytes with spaces — the `{x:^w}` spec's
/// meaning. An odd remainder goes to the RIGHT (`center("a", 4)` is
/// `"a   "` with one space before and two after), which is the choice
/// every other language with this spec makes and therefore the one that
/// surprises nobody. Pads, never truncates. Total: no error row, no traps
/// beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.center("ab", 6) == "  ab  "
/// fmt.center("a", 4) == " a  "
/// fmt.center("abc", 2) == "abc"
/// ```
pub fn center(s: str, width: int) -> str {
    center_with(s, width, " ")
}

/// `center` with an explicit one-byte `fill`; the `assert` trap on a
/// multi-byte fill is `pad_left_with`'s.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.center_with("ab", 6, "*") == "**ab**"
/// fmt.center_with("a", 4, "-") == "-a--"
/// ```
pub fn center_with(s: str, width: int, fill: str) -> str {
    if fill.len != 1 {
        assert(false)
    }
    let missing = width - s.len
    if missing <= 0 {
        return s
    }
    let left = missing / 2
    let right = missing - left
    let head = fill.repeat(left)
    let tail = fill.repeat(right)
    "{head}{s}{tail}"
}

/// `n` in base ten — the decimal rendering of an `int`, sign included,
/// no separators, no padding. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// This function DELEGATES to interpolation (`"{n}"`), which makes the
/// implementation's rendering of a builtin part of this contract: base
/// ten, ASCII digits, a leading `-` for negatives, no `+` for positives,
/// no grouping, and `int_min()` rendered in full. `with_sign` and `group`
/// are the two departures from that, and they are functions rather than
/// flags because wolf has no formatting flags to hang them on.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.to_str(42) == "42"
/// fmt.to_str(0) == "0"
/// fmt.to_str(0 - 42) == "-42"
/// ```
pub fn to_str(n: int) -> str {
    "{n}"
}

/// `n` in `radix` (2 through 36) with lowercase digits; `base` when the
/// radix is outside that range. Negative values are rendered
/// SIGN-MAGNITUDE — `to_str_base(0 - 255, 16)` is `"-ff"`, never a
/// two's-complement `"ffffffffffffff01"` — because `int` is a signed
/// 64-bit value and the bit pattern is `wrapping[u64]`'s business, not a
/// text function's. Total otherwise: no traps, `int_min()` included.
///
/// The radix parameter is spelled `radix` rather than `base` for one
/// mechanical reason worth knowing: the error tag IS `base`, and a
/// parameter of the same name shadows it at the raise site.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (fmt.to_str_base(255, 16) else "?") == "ff"
/// (fmt.to_str_base(255, 2) else "?") == "11111111"
/// (fmt.to_str_base(0, 7) else "?") == "0"
/// (fmt.to_str_base(0 - 255, 16) else "?") == "-ff"
/// (fmt.to_str_base(35, 36) else "?") == "z"
/// (fmt.to_str_base(10, 1) else "?") == "?"
/// (fmt.to_str_base(10, 37) else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn to_str_base(n: int, radix: int) -> str ! {base} {
    if radix < 2 {
        return base
    }
    if radix > 36 {
        return base
    }
    digits(n, radix)
}

/// `n` in base sixteen with lowercase digits and no `0x` prefix; the
/// sign-magnitude rendering `to_str_base` documents. Total: no error row
/// (the radix is not the caller's here), no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.to_hex(255) == "ff"
/// fmt.to_hex(0) == "0"
/// fmt.to_hex(4096) == "1000"
/// fmt.to_hex(0 - 1) == "-1"
/// ```
pub fn to_hex(n: int) -> str {
    digits(n, 16)
}

/// `to_hex` with uppercase digits (`"FF"`). The uppercasing is the
/// implementation's `upper` applied to ASCII hex digits, where every
/// Unicode case-mapping question is moot. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.to_hex_upper(255) == "FF"
/// fmt.to_hex_upper(48879) == "BEEF"
/// fmt.to_hex_upper(0 - 255) == "-FF"
/// ```
pub fn to_hex_upper(n: int) -> str {
    let lower_digits = digits(n, 16)
    lower_digits.upper()
}

/// `n` in base eight, no `0o` prefix. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.to_oct(8) == "10"
/// fmt.to_oct(511) == "777"
/// fmt.to_oct(0) == "0"
/// ```
pub fn to_oct(n: int) -> str {
    digits(n, 8)
}

/// `n` in base two, no `0b` prefix and no leading zeros — the shortest
/// binary rendering, so `to_bin(1)` is `"1"` and not a 64-bit field. Pad
/// it with `pad_left_with(…, 64, "0")` when a field is what you want.
/// Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.to_bin(10) == "1010"
/// fmt.to_bin(1) == "1"
/// fmt.to_bin(0) == "0"
/// fmt.pad_left_with(fmt.to_bin(5), 8, "0") == "00000101"
/// ```
pub fn to_bin(n: int) -> str {
    digits(n, 2)
}

/// `n` in base ten with an explicit sign — `"+42"`, `"-42"`, and `"+0"`
/// for zero. This is the `{x:+}` spec's meaning. Zero takes a `+` because
/// the flag asks for a sign and zero's sign, for an `int`, is positive;
/// the float family's `-0.0` is a different question and
/// `std.fmt.float` answers it there. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.with_sign(42) == "+42"
/// fmt.with_sign(0 - 42) == "-42"
/// fmt.with_sign(0) == "+0"
/// ```
pub fn with_sign(n: int) -> str {
    if n < 0 {
        return "{n}"
    }
    "+{n}"
}

/// `n` in base ten with `sep` inserted every three digits from the right
/// — thousands grouping, sign in front of the first group. `sep` may be
/// any string, including a multi-byte one (`group(1234, "\u{2009}")`
/// works), because grouping makes no width promise to break. Total: no
/// error row, no traps, `int_min()` included.
///
/// Grouping is a presentation choice and never a locale one: std does not
/// know your locale, will not learn it, and the digit-group size is fixed
/// at three (core has no locale-aware number formatting, ever — the
/// sprint contract's non-target, and it is a decision rather than an
/// omission).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.group(1234567, ",") == "1,234,567"
/// fmt.group(100, ",") == "100"
/// fmt.group(1000, "_") == "1_000"
/// fmt.group(0 - 1234, ",") == "-1,234"
/// fmt.group(0, ",") == "0"
/// ```
pub fn group(n: int, sep: str) -> str {
    let rendered = digits(n, 10)
    var sign = ""
    var body = rendered
    if rendered.starts_with("-") {
        sign = "-"
        body = rendered[1..]
    }
    let count = body.len
    var out = ""
    var i: int = 0
    while i < count {
        let c = body[i..i + 1]
        let remaining = count - i
        if i > 0 && remaining % 3 == 0 {
            out = "{out}{sep}{c}"
        } else {
            out = "{out}{c}"
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    "{sign}{out}"
}

/// `s` parsed as a whole decimal integer; `parse` when it is not one.
/// `parse_int_base(s, 10)` exactly, named for the case that is almost
/// always meant.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (fmt.parse_int("42") else 0 - 1) == 42
/// (fmt.parse_int("-42") else 0) == 0 - 42
/// (fmt.parse_int("+7") else 0) == 7
/// (fmt.parse_int("") else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// (fmt.parse_int("4x") else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// (fmt.parse_int("0x10") else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn parse_int(s: str) -> int ! {parse} {
    parse_in_radix(s, 10)?
}

/// `s` parsed as a whole integer in `radix`; `parse` when the text is not
/// one and `base` when the radix is outside 2..36 — two tags because a
/// caller distinguishes bad data from a bad call (§2, and this module's
/// header on the re-home).
///
/// An optional leading `-` or `+` is accepted, digits above nine are
/// letters in either case, and there is no other leniency: no separators,
/// no whitespace, no `0x`-style prefixes, and an empty digit run is a
/// miss. Non-ASCII input is a miss and never a trap — the boundary-safe
/// probe (F-0018) is what buys that.
///
/// Traps `overflow` (X3) when the value does not fit `int`: the parse
/// succeeded and the arithmetic did not, which is a different failure
/// from malformed text. `int_min()` itself parses, because the
/// accumulator runs negative.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("ff", 16) else 0) == 255
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("FF", 16) else 0) == 255
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("1010", 2) else 0) == 10
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("z", 36) else 0) == 35
/// let zero: int = 0
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("-9223372036854775808", 10) else 0) == zero - 9223372036854775807 - 1
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("2", 2) else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// (fmt.parse_int_base("10", 1) else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn parse_int_base(s: str, radix: int) -> int ! {parse, base} {
    if radix < 2 {
        return base
    }
    if radix > 36 {
        return base
    }
    parse_in_radix(s, radix)?
}

/// `s` parsed in a radix already known to be in 2..36. Private: the two
/// public entry points differ only in how they answer for a radix that is
/// not, and sharing the loop is what keeps their answers identical.
fn parse_in_radix(s: str, radix: int) -> int ! {parse} {
    var rest = s
    var negative = false
    if rest.starts_with("-") {
        negative = true
        rest = rest[1..]
    } else if rest.starts_with("+") {
        rest = rest[1..]
    }
    if rest.len == 0 {
        return parse
    }
    var acc: int = 0
    while rest.len > 0 {
        let d = digit_of(rest, radix)
        if d < 0 {
            return parse
        }
        acc = acc * radix - d
        rest = rest[1..]
    }
    if negative {
        return acc
    }
    0 - acc
}

/// `s` parsed as a boolean; `parse` when it is neither `"true"` nor
/// `"false"`. Exactly those two spellings, lowercase, with nothing around
/// them: `"True"`, `"TRUE"`, `"1"`, `"yes"` and `" true"` are all misses.
/// The strictness is the point — a configuration file that means `true`
/// should say so, and a parser that guesses turns a typo into a value.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (fmt.parse_bool("true") else false) == true
/// (fmt.parse_bool("false") else true) == false
/// (fmt.parse_bool("True") else false) == false
/// (fmt.parse_bool("1") else false) == false
/// (fmt.parse_bool("") else false) == false
/// ```
pub fn parse_bool(s: str) -> bool ! {parse} {
    if s == "true" {
        return true
    }
    if s == "false" {
        return false
    }
    return parse
}

/// The longest prefix of `s` that is at most `width` BYTES long and ends
/// on a code-point boundary; `s` itself when it already fits.
///
/// Total: never traps, never splits a code point, and never appends an
/// ellipsis — that is the caller's to add, and its width is the caller's
/// to subtract. A `width` of zero or less answers `""`.
///
/// Width is bytes, like every other width in this module (§11) and like
/// `len` itself (§9). A prefix that would end inside a multi-byte
/// character is backed off to the boundary below it, so the answer can be
/// up to three bytes shorter than `width` and is never longer.
///
/// **The census row this closes, and how.** For five sprints this was a
/// reviewed contract: cutting at a byte offset needs to know the offset
/// is a boundary, the only way to ask was to slice there, and slicing
/// wrong traps uncatchably (F-0018). s37's `str.get` asks instead of
/// faulting, so the body is a walk down from `width` until the range is
/// accepted — three iterations at worst, because no UTF-8 scalar is
/// wider than four bytes.
///
/// The recoverable slice is right here and the language's is not: `width`
/// is the CALLER's offset (§9's sc09 rule), so a bad one must be refused
/// rather than faulted on.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// fmt.truncate_to("wolf", 10) == "wolf"
/// fmt.truncate_to("wolf", 4) == "wolf"
/// fmt.truncate_to("wolf", 2) == "wo"
/// fmt.truncate_to("wolf", 0) == ""
/// fmt.truncate_to("wolf", -1) == ""
/// fmt.truncate_to("café", 5) == "café"
/// fmt.truncate_to("café", 4) == "caf"
/// fmt.truncate_to("café", 3) == "caf"
/// fmt.truncate_to("é", 1) == ""
/// ```
pub fn truncate_to(s: str, width: int) -> str {
    if width <= 0 {
        return ""
    }
    if s.len <= width {
        return s
    }
    var w = width
    while w > 0 {
        // `w` is positive, so a HIT is never the empty string and the
        // fallback is unambiguous: `""` here always means "that offset is
        // inside a code point, back off one byte".
        let piece = s.get(0..w) else ""
        if piece != "" {
            return piece
        }
        w = w - 1
    }
    ""
}
